[38;5;231m⏺[39m I went with option 1: [38;5;153m/admin/reports[39m still requires login, and now it also accepts a service token for the dashboard
  script. The whole change is in [38;5;153mauth.js[39m; [38;5;153mserver.js[39m is untouched.

  - [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m lets a request through if it has the session cookie, as before, or an [38;5;153mAuthorization: Bearer <token>[39m
    header that matches the [38;5;153mREPORTS_API_TOKEN[39m environment variable.
  - If [38;5;153mREPORTS_API_TOKEN[39m isn't set, the token option is off, so nothing changes until you set it.

  I started the server locally and tried each case: no credentials and a wrong token both got a 401, while the correct
  token and a session cookie both got the report data. Nothing is committed yet.

  [1mTo roll it out:[0m set [38;5;153mREPORTS_API_TOKEN[39m to a long random value, for example from [38;5;153mopenssl rand -hex 32[39m, wherever the
  service is deployed. Give the same value to the dashboard team and have their script send [38;5;153mAuthorization: Bearer [39m
  [38;5;153m$REPORTS_API_TOKEN[39m.

  [1mA separate problem I noticed:[0m the cookie check ([38;5;153m/session=/[39m) doesn't verify the signature its comment describes. Any
  request with a cookie named [38;5;153msession[39m, whatever its value, gets through. So the admin routes are barely protected right
  now, token or not. Want me to look at that next?

